\date{2026-02-11T11:59:06Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Prove that #{(2n + 1 > 6) \to (n > 2)}.}

\proof{
  To prove #{(2n + 1 > 6) \to (n > 2)}, suppose #{(2n + 1 > 6)}, then we must show #{(n > 2)}.
  \subproof{
    Starting from #{(2n + 1 > 6)} we can reason as follows:

    \p{
    \stmt{2n+1 > 6}
    \reason{\to}{subtract 1 from both sides}
    \stmt{2n > 5}
    \reason{\to}{divide both sides by 2}
    \stmt{n > 5/2}
    \reason{\to}{#{5/2 > 2}, and #{>} is transitive}
    \stmt{n > 2}
    }
  }
  Since we have shown that #{n > 2} must be true under the assumption that #{2n + 1 > 6}, therefore #{(2n+1 > 6) \to (n > 2)}.
}
